carpal tunnel. Clinical Features • Numbness/tingling in thumb, index, middle and radial half of ring finger. • Night symptoms. • Hand weakness. • Dropping objects. • Difficulty with fine motor activities. • Thenar muscle wasting in advanced cases. • Reduced thumb opposition. • Positive Phalen test. • Positive Tinel sign may occur. Investigations • Nerve conduction studies/EMG. • Ultrasound. • Imaging if structural pathology is suspected. Management • Activity modification. • Neutral wrist splint, especially at night. • Analgesics. • Corticosteroid injection in selected cases. • Median nerve-gliding and tendon-gliding exercises. • Ergonomic correction. • Wrist ROM. • Carpal tunnel release for severe or persistent cases. 10. Dupuytren's Contracture Definition Dupuytren's contracture is a progressive fibrotic disorder of the palmar fascia, producing nodules and cords that cause flexion contractures of the fingers, most commonly ring and little fingers. Clinical Features • Palmar nodules. • Palmar cords. • Progressive finger flexion contracture. • Difficulty placing hand flat on a table. • Difficulty washing face, wearing gloves or putting hand into pockets. Page 15 BPT University Exam Notes• Usually painless. • Reduced hand function. Management • Observation in mild disease. • Splinting in selected cases. • ROM, stretching and functional training. • Postoperative rehabilitation. • Needle fasciotomy/collagenase treatment in selected settings. • Surgical fasciectomy for significant contracture. PELVIS AND HIP 11. Iliotibial Band Syndrome Definition IT band syndrome is an overuse condition causing pain around the lateral aspect of the knee, resulting from irritation of tissues between the iliotibial band and lateral femoral region during repetitive knee flexion and extension. Causes • Running. • Cycling. • Repetitive knee flexion-extension. • Sudden increase in training. • Poor biomechanics. • Weak hip abductors. Clinical Features • Lateral knee pain. • Pain during running, especially downhill. • Pain worsens with repetitive activity. • Tenderness over lateral femoral epicondyle. • Pain around 20–30° of knee flexion may occur. • Tight IT band. • Hip abductor weakness. Management • Relative rest. • Ice. Page 16 BPT University Exam Notes• Activity modification. • Correct training errors. • Stretching. • Hip abductor and gluteal strengthening. • Core strengthening. • Running biomechanics correction. • Gradual return to sport. 12. Piriformis Syndrome Definition Piriformis syndrome is a condition in which the piriformis muscle irritates or compresses the sciatic nerve, producing buttock pain with possible radiating symptoms. Clinical Features • Deep buttock pain. • Pain may radiate down posterior thigh. • Pain aggravated by prolonged sitting. • Pain during running/walking. • Tenderness over piriformis. • Pain with hip flexion, adduction and internal rotation. • Possible paresthesia. • Reduced tolerance for sitting. Management • Activity modification. • Ice/heat as appropriate. • Analgesics. • Piriformis stretching. • Hip mobility exercises. • Gluteal and core strengthening. • Neural mobilization when appropriate. • Postural correction. • Gradual return to activity. 13. Trochanteric Bursitis / Greater Trochanteric Pain Syndrome Definition Trochanteric bursitis is pain around the greater trochanter, traditionally attributed to inflammation of the trochanteric bursa. Clinically, lateral hip pain often represents greater trochanteric pain syndrome, which can also involve the gluteal tendons. Page 17 BPT University Exam NotesClinical Features • Lateral hip pain. • Tenderness over greater trochanter. • Pain when lying on affected side. • Pain during walking/running. • Difficulty climbing stairs. • Pain during prolonged standing. • Pain with resisted hip abduction. • Possible IT band tightness. Management • Avoid prolonged pressure on affected side. • Activity modification. • Ice. • Analgesics/NSAIDs. • Hip abductor, gluteal and pelvic stabilization exercises. • Flexibility work where appropriate. • Gait retraining. • Gradual functional progression. • Injection may be considered in selected cases. KNEE 14. Osteochondritis Dissecans Definition Osteochondritis dissecans is a condition in which a segment of subchondral bone and its overlying articular cartilage becomes damaged and may become partially or completely separated from the surrounding bone. Common Site Lateral aspect of medial femoral condyle is common. Causes/Risk Factors • Repetitive microtrauma. • Sports activities. • Abnormal joint loading. • Vascular factors may contribute. Page 18 BPT University Exam NotesClinical Features • Activity-related knee pain. • Swelling/effusion. • Tenderness. • Catching or locking in unstable lesions. • Reduced ROM. • Limp. • Quadriceps weakness. • Mechanical symptoms in advanced disease. Investigations • X-ray. • MRI. • CT in selected cases. Management • Activity restriction. • Avoid impact loading. • Physiotherapy. • Maintain ROM. • Quadriceps and hip strengthening. • Surgical options in selected lesions: drilling, fixation, debridement and osteochondral grafting. 15. Prepatellar Bursitis Definition Prepatellar bursitis is inflammation of the bursa located between the patella and overlying skin, commonly caused by repetitive kneeling. It is also called housemaid's knee. Clinical Features • Swelling directly over patella. • Anterior knee pain/tenderness. • Fluctuant swelling. • Pain with kneeling. • Redness/warmth in acute inflammation or infection. • Knee joint ROM may be relatively preserved. Management • Avoid kneeling. Page 19 BPT University Exam Notes• Knee padding. • Ice. • Compression. • Analgesics. • Aspiration when indicated. • Antibiotics for septic bursitis. • Physiotherapy after acute symptoms settle. • Strengthening and functional rehabilitation. 16. Suprapatellar Bursitis Definition Suprapatellar bursitis is inflammation of the suprapatellar bursa, located above the patella and communicating with the knee joint in many individuals. Clinical Features • Swelling above patella. • Anterior knee pain. • Tenderness. • Pain during knee flexion. • Possible joint effusion. • Reduced knee movement due to pain/swelling. Management • Rest/activity modification. • Ice. • Compression. • Analgesics/NSAIDs. • Treatment of underlying inflammatory/infective cause. • ROM exercises. • Quadriceps strengthening. • Gradual functional training. 17. Popliteal Tendinitis Definition Popliteal tendinitis is an overuse injury of the popliteus tendon, producing posterolateral knee pain. Clinical Features • Posterolateral knee pain. Page 20 BPT University Exam Notes• Pain during running, especially downhill. • Pain during knee flexion/rotation. • Tenderness around posterolateral knee. • Pain may increase with weight-bearing. • Possible feeling of instability. Management • Relative rest. • Ice. • Activity modification. • Analgesics as prescribed. • Gentle ROM. • Hamstring and calf flexibility. • Progressive strengthening of knee and hip musculature. • Proprioceptive training. • Gradual return to running/sport. 18. Patellar Tendinitis Definition Patellar tendinitis/tendinopathy is an overuse disorder of the patellar tendon, commonly at its attachment to the inferior pole of the patella. It is commonly called jumper's knee. Causes • Jumping sports. • Running. • Repetitive knee extension. • Sudden increase in training. • Poor load management. Clinical Features • Anterior knee pain. • Localized tenderness at inferior pole of patella. • Pain during jumping, running and squatting. • Pain during stairs. • Pain during resisted knee extension. • Reduced functional performance. Management • Activity modification and load management. Page 21 BPT University Exam Notes• Ice for symptomatic relief. • Quadriceps stretching. • Progressive tendon loading. • Isometric quadriceps exercises initially. • Eccentric or heavy-slow resistance training. • Hip/core strengthening. • Functional and sports-specific rehabilitation. 19. Chondromalacia Patella Definition Chondromalacia patella refers to softening and degenerative changes of the articular cartilage on the posterior surface of the patella, commonly associated with patellofemoral pain and abnormal patellar loading. Clinical Features • Anterior knee pain. • Pain during stairs, squatting, running, jumping or prolonged sitting. • Crepitus. • Patellar tenderness. • Pain with appropriate patellar compression testing. • Possible quadriceps and hip muscle weakness. Management • Activity modification. • Ice. • Analgesics if prescribed. • Quadriceps strengthening. • Hip abductors/external rotator strengthening. • Stretching of tight lower-limb muscles. • Patellar taping in selected patients. • Movement retraining. • Gradual return to activity. 20. Plica Syndrome Definition Plica syndrome is a symptomatic condition caused by irritation or thickening of a synovial plica, usually the medial plica of the knee. Clinical Features Page 22 BPT University Exam Notes• Anterior/anteromedial knee pain. • Clicking or snapping. • Pain during repetitive knee flexion-extension. • Tenderness along medial patellar border. • Pain during stairs/squatting. • Possible catching sensation. • Symptoms may increase after prolonged activity. Management • Activity modification. • Ice. • Analgesics. • Quadriceps and hip strengthening. • Stretching. • Patellar tracking/movement retraining. • Gradual return to activity. • Arthroscopic excision if persistent conservative treatment fails. 21. Hoffa's Fat Pad Syndrome Definition Hoffa's fat pad syndrome is a painful condition involving irritation, inflammation or impingement of the infrapatellar (Hoffa's) fat pad, located behind the patellar tendon and below the patella. Causes • Repetitive hyperextension. • Direct trauma. • Patellar maltracking. • Repetitive loading. • Previous knee surgery. Clinical Features • Anterior knee pain. • Pain around either side of patellar tendon. • Pain aggravated by knee extension/hyperextension. • Tenderness around infrapatellar region. • Swelling/fullness. • Pain during prolonged standing or activity. • Possible catching. Page 23 BPT University Exam NotesManagement • Avoid painful hyperextension. • Activity modification. • Ice. • Analgesics as prescribed. • Quadriceps and hip strengthening. • Patellar control. • Movement retraining. • Flexibility exercises. • Taping may be useful in selected patients. • Persistent cases require orthopedic assessment. ANKLE AND FOOT 22. Ankle Sprains Definition An ankle sprain is an injury to one or more ligaments of the ankle caused by excessive or abnormal movement, most commonly inversion injury affecting the lateral ligaments. Commonly Injured Ligaments • Anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL). • Calcaneofibular ligament (CFL). • Posterior talofibular ligament. Clinical Features • Pain. • Swelling. • Tenderness. • Bruising. • Difficulty weight-bearing. • Reduced ROM. • Instability. • Weakness. • Severe injury may cause inability to walk normally. Grades Grade I: mild stretching/microscopic injury with mild pain and swelling. Grade II: partial ligament tear with moderate pain/swelling and some instability. Page 24 BPT University Exam NotesGrade III: complete rupture with severe swelling and instability. Management • Protection. • Relative rest. • Compression. • Elevation. • Early protected weight-bearing as tolerated. • Pain/swelling management. • Gentle ROM. • Progressive weight-bearing. • Calf and peroneal strengthening. • Proprioception and balance training. • Agility and sport-specific rehabilitation. • Bracing/taping where indicated. 23. Plantar Fasciitis / Calcaneal Spur Definition Plantar fasciitis is a degenerative/overuse condition of the plantar fascia, commonly producing pain at its medial calcaneal attachment. A calcaneal spur is a bony projection from the calcaneus and may be present with or without plantar fasciitis; the spur itself is not necessarily the cause of pain. Risk Factors • Prolonged standing. • Running. • Obesity. • Tight calf muscles. • Pes planus or abnormal foot mechanics. • Sudden increase in activity. • Inappropriate footwear. Clinical Features • Heel pain, especially plantar-medial heel. • First-step pain after waking. • Pain after prolonged sitting/rest. • Pain after prolonged standing/walking. • Tenderness at medial calcaneal tubercle. • Tight calf/Achilles complex. • Altered gait. Page 25 BPT University Exam NotesManagement • Activity modification. • Ice. • Appropriate footwear. • Heel cushioning. • Calf stretching. • Plantar fascia stretching. • Foot intrinsic strengthening. • Calf strengthening. • Orthotics where indicated. • Taping. • Night splint in selected cases. • Progressive loading. • Weight management where appropriate. • Persistent cases may require specialist treatment. 24. Tarsal Tunnel Syndrome Definition Tarsal tunnel syndrome is a compressive neuropathy of the tibial nerve or its branches as it passes through the tarsal tunnel behind the medial malleolus. Causes • Trauma. • Ankle sprain. • Space-occupying lesion. • Ganglion. • Diabetes. • Inflammatory conditions. • Foot deformity. • Repetitive loading. Clinical Features • Pain around medial ankle. • Burning/tingling. • Numbness in plantar foot. • Symptoms may radiate toward sole/toes. • Worse with prolonged standing/walking. • Night symptoms may occur. Page 26 BPT University Exam Notes• Positive Tinel sign behind medial malleolus. • Possible intrinsic foot muscle weakness. Investigations • Clinical examination. • Nerve conduction studies/EMG. • Ultrasound/MRI where appropriate. Management • Activity modification. • Appropriate footwear. • Orthotics if indicated. • Analgesics. • Neural mobilization. • Calf stretching. • Foot strengthening. • Gait correction. • Proprioceptive training. • Treatment of underlying cause. • Surgical decompression for persistent/severe compression. 25. Achilles Tendinitis / Achilles Tendinopathy Definition Achilles tendinopathy is a painful overuse disorder of the Achilles tendon, usually involving degenerative changes associated with repetitive mechanical loading. Causes/Risk Factors • Running and jumping. • Sudden increase in training. • Poor training progression. • Tight calf muscles. • Inappropriate footwear. • Reduced ankle dorsiflexion. • Previous tendon problems. Clinical Features • Pain at Achilles tendon. • Mid-portion or insertional pain. • Morning stiffness. Page 27 BPT University Exam Notes• Pain during running/jumping. • Tenderness. • Tendon thickening may occur. • Pain during resisted plantarflexion. • Reduced calf strength. • Difficulty with repeated heel raises. Management • Relative rest/load modification. • Ice for symptom relief. • Appropriate footwear. • Heel lift temporarily in selected cases. • Calf stretching where appropriate. • Progressive tendon loading. • Isometric exercises initially when painful. • Heavy-slow resistance/eccentric loading. • Calf strengthening. • Balance and functional training. • Gradual return to running/jumping. 26. Metatarsalgia Definition Metatarsalgia is pain arising from the forefoot/metatarsal head region, usually caused by excessive loading of the metatarsal heads. Causes • Excessive running/jumping. • Improper footwear. • High heels. • Pes cavus. • Hallux valgus. • Altered foot biomechanics. • Increased body weight. • Fat-pad changes. Clinical Features • Pain under metatarsal heads. • Burning or aching forefoot pain. • Pain worsens with walking/running. Page 28 BPT University Exam Notes• Feeling of walking on a pebble may occur. • Callus formation. • Tenderness. • Reduced tolerance for prolonged standing. Management • Activity modification. • Proper footwear. • Avoid high heels. • Metatarsal pads. • Orthotics when indicated. • Ice. • Calf and foot stretching. • Foot intrinsic strengthening. • Gait correction. • Gradual return to activity. • Treat underlying foot deformity. 27. Morton's Neuroma Definition Morton's neuroma is a painful perineural fibrotic enlargement/irritation of a common digital nerve, most commonly occurring between the third and fourth toes. Causes/Risk Factors • Narrow footwear. • High heels. • Repetitive forefoot loading. • Running. • Foot deformities. Clinical Features • Burning forefoot pain. • Pain radiating toward toes. • Numbness/tingling. • Feeling of a pebble or lump inside the shoe. • Symptoms worsen with tight shoes. • Pain increases during walking/running. • Tenderness in interdigital space. • Clicking may be produced by compression of the forefoot (Mulder sign). Page 29 BPT University Exam NotesInvestigations • Clinical examination. • Ultrasound. • MRI in selected cases. Management • Wide toe-box footwear. • Avoid high heels. • Metatarsal pads. • Activity modification. • Orthotics where indicated. • Analgesics. • Foot intrinsic strengthening. • Calf stretching. • Foot mobility. • Gait correction. • Injection may be considered. • Surgical decompression/neurectomy in persistent cases.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-13T16:11:15+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\n<USER_SETTINGS_CHANGE>\nThe user changed setting `Model Selection` from Gemini 3.1 Pro (High) to Gemini 3.1 Pro (Low). No need to comment on this change if the user doesn't ask about it. If reporting what model you are, please use a human readable name instead of the exact string.\n</USER_SETTINGS_CHANGE>"}
{"step_index":8,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T10:41:15Z","content":"**PART I SHOULDER Regional Conditions of the Shoulder 20 Mark University Exam Answer** **Introduction** The shoulder is a highly mobile joint complex consisting mainly of the glenohumeral acromioclavicular sternoclavicular and scapulothoracic articulations Because of its wide range of movement and dependence on soft tissues for stability it is commonly affected by painful disorders involving the joint capsule rotator cuff tendons biceps tendon and subacromial bursa The important regional shoulder conditions include 1 Periarthritis shoulder / Adhesive capsulitis 2 Rotator cuff tendinitis 3 Supraspinatus tendinitis 4 Infraspinatus tendinitis 5 Bicipital tendinitis 6 Subacromial bursitis --- **1 Periarthritis Shoulder / Adhesive Capsulitis** **Definition** Adhesive capsulitis is a painful condition of the shoulder characterized by progressive pain stiffness and marked restriction of both active and passive glenohumeral movements particularly external rotation It is commonly called frozen shoulder **Clinical Features** • Gradual onset of shoulder pain • Pain is often worse at night and may disturb sleep • Progressive stiffness of the shoulder • Difficulty with combing hair dressing reaching overhead and reaching behind the back • Both active and passive ROM are restricted • External rotation is usually the most restricted movement followed by abduction and internal rotation • Capsular pattern External rotation abduction internal rotation • Muscle weakness may develop secondary to pain and disuse • Scapular movements may become excessive as compensation **Stages** 1 Freezing stage increasing pain and progressive loss of movement 2 Frozen stage pain may decrease but marked stiffness persists 3 Thawing stage gradual recovery of ROM and function **Management** **Medical management** • Patient education and activity modification • Analgesics and NSAIDs as prescribed • Corticosteroid injection particularly in the painful early stage • Hydrodilatation in selected patients • Manipulation under anesthesia or arthroscopic capsular release in resistant cases **Physiotherapy management** • Pain relieving modalities as appropriate • Gentle pendular exercises • Gentle active assisted ROM • Avoid aggressive stretching that increases pain • Joint mobilization and capsular stretching • Active and active assisted ROM • Wand / pulley exercises • Scapular mobility exercises • Progressive stretching and strengthening • Rotator cuff and scapular muscle strengthening • Functional and home exercise programme --- **2 Rotator Cuff Tendinitis** **Definition** Rotator cuff tendinitis is an inflammatory or degenerative condition involving one or more tendons of the supraspinatus infraspinatus teres minor and subscapularis commonly producing shoulder pain during elevation and rotation The supraspinatus tendon is most frequently involved **Clinical Features** • Pain over the shoulder commonly around the anterolateral aspect • Pain increases with overhead activities • Painful arc during shoulder abduction commonly between 60 120 • Pain may increase at night • Weakness due to pain and inhibition • Restricted active movement may occur • Passive ROM is usually relatively preserved • Tenderness over the affected tendon • Positive rotator cuff provocation tests may be present • Difficulty with lifting reaching and overhead activities **Management** • Relative rest and activity modification • Avoid repetitive overhead activities • Ice during the painful phase • Analgesics / NSAIDs as prescribed • Gentle ROM and pendulum exercises • Stretching • Rotator cuff strengthening • Scapular stabilizer strengthening • Progressive resisted exercises • Postural correction • Gradual return to overhead activities • Specialist assessment for persistent symptoms or structural pathology --- **3 Supraspinatus Tendinitis** **Definition** Supraspinatus tendinitis is an inflammatory or degenerative disorder of the supraspinatus tendon usually occurring near its passage beneath the acromion causing pain particularly during shoulder elevation **Clinical Features** • Pain over the anterolateral aspect of the shoulder • Pain increases during abduction and overhead activities • Painful arc commonly from approximately 60 120 • Pain may be worse at night • Tenderness over the supraspinatus tendon • Pain or weakness during resisted abduction • Difficulty lifting the arm • Positive empty can / Jobe test may reproduce pain or weakness • Passive ROM is generally less affected than active ROM • Severe cases may produce weakness of abduction **Management** • Relative rest from painful overhead activities • Ice during acute painful periods • Analgesics / NSAIDs as prescribed • Gentle ROM exercises • Stretching • Progressive supraspinatus and rotator cuff strengthening • Isometric isotonic functional strengthening • Scapular stabilization exercises • Postural correction • Gradual return to overhead activities • Corticosteroid injection may be considered in selected cases • Persistent or associated structural lesions may require specialist assessment --- **4 Infraspinatus Tendinitis** **Definition** Infraspinatus tendinitis is inflammation or degeneration of the infraspinatus tendon resulting in pain and dysfunction mainly during external rotation of the shoulder **Clinical Features** • Posterior shoulder pain • Pain may radiate toward the lateral arm • Pain increases during external rotation • Pain during overhead activities • Tenderness over the posterior shoulder / infraspinatus tendon • Pain or weakness on resisted external rotation • Difficulty with activities requiring external rotation • Weakness may occur because of pain • Passive ROM is usually relatively preserved **Management** • Relative rest and avoidance of aggravating activities • Ice for pain where appropriate • Analgesics / NSAIDs as prescribed • Gentle shoulder ROM • Stretching exercises • Isometric external rotation exercises initially • Progressive resisted external rotation • Rotator cuff and scapular stabilizer strengthening • Postural correction • Gradual functional and sports specific rehabilitation --- **5 Bicipital Tendinitis** **Definition** Bicipital tendinitis is inflammation or degenerative irritation of the tendon of the long head of the biceps brachii particularly within the intertubercular / bicipital groove **Clinical Features** • Pain in the anterior shoulder • Pain may radiate down the anterior aspect of the arm • Pain increases with shoulder flexion elbow flexion forearm supination and overhead activities • Tenderness over the bicipital groove • Pain on resisted elbow flexion • Pain on resisted forearm supination • Positive Speeds test may reproduce symptoms • Yergasons test may also reproduce pain • Weakness may occur due to pain • Night pain can occur • Difficulty lifting objects or performing repetitive overhead activities **Management** • Relative rest and activity modification • Avoid repetitive lifting and overhead activities • Ice during painful periods • Analgesics / NSAIDs as prescribed • Gentle shoulder ROM and stretching • Progressive biceps strengthening after pain decreases • Rotator cuff and scapular stabilization • Postural correction • Gradual return to functional activities • Injection or surgical management may be considered depending on underlying pathology --- **6 Subacromial Bursitis** **Definition** Subacromial bursitis is inflammation of the subacromial bursa a fluid filled structure that reduces friction between the rotator cuff tendons and the acromion during shoulder movement **Clinical Features** • Shoulder pain commonly over the lateral / anterior shoulder • Pain increases with overhead activities • Painful arc often around 60 120 of abduction • Night pain may be present • Tenderness around the subacromial region • Pain with active shoulder elevation • Difficulty reaching overhead • Difficulty dressing and grooming • Muscle guarding may occur • Weakness may be present due to pain inhibition **Management** **Medical management** • Relative rest • Activity modification • Ice • Analgesics / NSAIDs as prescribed • Corticosteroid injection may be considered in selected cases **Physiotherapy management** • Pain relieving modalities when indicated • Gentle ROM and pendular exercises • Stretching • Rotator cuff and scapular stabilizer strengthening • Postural correction • Correction of movement patterns • Progressive functional exercises • Gradual return to overhead activities --- **Comparison of Important Clinical Features** • Adhesive capsulitis joint capsule restriction of both active and passive ROM • Rotator cuff tendinitis rotator cuff tendons pain with elevation / overhead activity • Supraspinatus tendinitis supraspinatus tendon painful arc and painful resisted abduction • Infraspinatus tendinitis infraspinatus tendon pain with resisted external rotation • Bicipital tendinitis long head of biceps anterior shoulder pain and bicipital groove tenderness • Subacromial bursitis subacromial bursa painful elevation / abduction --- **General Physiotherapy Principles** 1 Pain management cryotherapy where appropriate heat in selected chronic / stiff conditions adjunctive electrotherapy where indicated and activity modification 2 Restoration of ROM pendular active assisted wand and pulley exercises capsular stretching in adhesive capsulitis 3 Strengthening isometric isotonic resisted functional strengthening 4 Scapular rehabilitation retraction depression controlled upward rotation serratus anterior and lower trapezius strengthening 5 Postural correction 6 Functional rehabilitation ADLs overhead activity work specific and sports specific exercises --- **Conclusion** Regional shoulder conditions commonly involve the capsule rotator cuff tendons biceps tendon and subacromial bursa Careful assessment of pain location painful arc active and passive ROM resisted movements and special tests helps differentiate them Management is primarily conservative and includes pain control activity modification restoration of mobility progressive strengthening scapular stabilization postural correction and functional rehabilitation --- **PART II ELBOW WRIST AND HAND PELVIS AND HIP KNEE ANKLE AND FOOT Regional Orthopaedic Conditions 20 Mark University Examination Answers** --- **ELBOW** --- **1 Tennis Elbow Lateral Epicondylitis** **Definition** Tennis elbow is a painful overuse disorder of the tendons originating from the common extensor origin at the lateral epicondyle of the humerus most commonly involving the extensor carpi radialis brevis It is usually a tendinopathy rather than a purely inflammatory condition **Etiology / Risk Factors** • Repetitive wrist extension • Repetitive gripping • Racquet sports • Manual work • Repetitive computer or occupational activities • Sudden increase in activity • Poor technique during sports **Clinical Features** • Pain over the lateral epicondyle sometimes radiating down the lateral forearm • Pain aggravated by gripping wrist extension lifting turning a screwdriver and shaking hands • Tenderness over the lateral epicondyle / common extensor origin • Reduced grip strength due to pain • Pain on resisted wrist extension • Difficulty performing occupational activities **Investigations** • Mainly clinical • X ray to exclude bony pathology • Ultrasound / MRI in persistent or unclear cases **Management** • Activity modification and relative rest • Ice during painful periods • Analgesics / NSAIDs as prescribed • Counterforce brace or wrist splint in selected cases • Wrist / forearm stretching • Isometric wrist extension • Progressive eccentric / concentric extensor strengthening • Grip strengthening • Manual therapy • Ergonomic correction • Gradual return to sport / work --- **2 Golfers Elbow Medial Epicondylitis** **Definition** Golfers elbow is a tendinopathy involving the common flexor pronator tendon origin at the medial epicondyle of the humerus producing medial elbow pain **Etiology** • Repetitive wrist flexion • Repetitive forearm pronation • Golf • Throwing sports • Manual occupations • Repetitive gripping **Clinical Features** • Pain over medial epicondyle possibly radiating down medial forearm • Tenderness at common flexor origin • Pain during wrist flexion forearm pronation and gripping • Reduced grip strength • Pain on resisted wrist flexion / pronation • Occasionally ulnar nerve symptoms may coexist **Management** • Activity modification ice and analgesics as prescribed • Brace if indicated • Stretching of wrist flexors • Isometric progressive resisted strengthening • Grip strengthening • Eccentric training • Manual therapy • Ergonomic correction • Gradual return to activity --- **3 Olecranon Bursitis Students Elbow** **Definition** Olecranon bursitis is inflammation of the olecranon bursa located between the skin and olecranon process of the ulna It is commonly called students elbow because prolonged leaning on the elbows can irritate the bursa **Causes** • Repeated pressure • Trauma • Prolonged elbow leaning • Infection • Gout or inflammatory arthritis **Clinical Features** • Swelling over posterior elbow • Soft / fluctuant swelling over olecranon • Pain or tenderness • Warmth and redness if inflamed / infected • Usually preserved elbow ROM • Septic cases may cause fever and systemic symptoms **Investigations** • Clinical examination • Aspiration and fluid analysis when infection / gout is suspected • Blood investigations in suspected infection • Imaging when necessary **Management** Non infective avoid pressure activity modification ice compression / padding analgesics and selected aspiration recurrent cases may require surgery Septic bursitis medical assessment antibiotics and drainage / aspiration when indicated Physiotherapy protection gentle ROM avoidance of direct pressure and gradual strengthening after acute symptoms settle --- **4 Triceps Tendinitis** **Definition** Triceps tendinitis is an overuse tendinopathy affecting the distal triceps tendon near its insertion into the olecranon causing posterior elbow pain **Causes** • Repetitive elbow extension • Weightlifting • Throwing • Repetitive pushing • Sudden increase in exercise load **Clinical Features** • Posterior elbow pain • Tenderness near triceps insertion • Pain during resisted elbow extension • Pain during pushing activities • Weakness due to pain • Pain after repetitive activity • Occasionally swelling **Management** • Relative rest and ice • Activity modification • Analgesics / NSAIDs • Gentle ROM • Triceps stretching • Isometric exercises initially • Progressive resisted strengthening • Eccentric loading • Gradual return to sport / work --- **WRIST AND HAND** --- **5 De Quervains Tenosynovitis** **Definition** De Quervains tenosynovitis is a painful stenosing tenosynovitis affecting the first dorsal compartment of the wrist involving the abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis tendons **Causes / Risk Factors** • Repetitive thumb movements • Repetitive wrist motion • Lifting infants • Occupational overuse • Repetitive gripping **Clinical Features** • Pain over radial side of wrist possibly radiating toward thumb / forearm • Swelling over first dorsal compartment • Pain with thumb movement • Pain during gripping and lifting • Reduced grip strength • Tenderness over radial styloid • Positive Finkelstein test **Management** • Activity modification • Thumb spica splint • Ice • Analgesics / NSAIDs • Corticosteroid injection when indicated • Gentle ROM thumb exercises tendon gliding stretching and progressive strengthening • Surgical release in persistent cases --- **6 Ganglion** **Definition** A ganglion is a benign cystic swelling containing gelatinous fluid commonly arising from a joint capsule or tendon sheath particularly around the wrist **Clinical Features** • Smooth rounded swelling commonly dorsal wrist • May change in size • May be painless • Pain may occur with wrist movement or pressure • Possible weakness or discomfort • Usually firm / fluctuant • May transilluminate **Investigations** • Clinical examination • Ultrasound • MRI when diagnosis is uncertain **Management** • Observation if asymptomatic • Activity modification • Splinting for painful cases • Aspiration in selected cases • Surgical excision for recurrent / symptomatic ganglion • Physiotherapy for pain ROM strengthening functional modification and scar management after surgery --- **7 Trigger Finger / Trigger Thumb** **Definition** Trigger finger is a stenosing tenosynovitis in which thickening of the flexor tendon / pulley system causes difficulty in smooth tendon gliding producing catching locking or snapping during finger movement **Clinical Features** • Pain / tenderness at volar MCP region • Clicking or snapping • Finger catches during flexion / extension • Locking in flexion in advanced cases • Morning stiffness • Palpable flexor tendon nodule • Difficulty opening / closing the hand **Management** • Activity modification • Rest • Splinting • Analgesics • Tendon gliding exercises • Gentle ROM • Corticosteroid injection when indicated • Surgical A1 pulley release for persistent / severe cases • Postoperative scar management and mobilization --- **8 Mallet Finger** **Definition** Mallet finger is an injury to the terminal extensor mechanism at the distal interphalangeal joint usually resulting in inability to actively extend the DIP joint **Causes** • Sudden forced flexion of an actively extended finger • Sports injuries • Avulsion fracture may occur **Clinical Features** • Drooping fingertip • Inability to actively extend DIP • Pain and swelling may occur • Passive extension is usually possible • Chronic cases may develop swan neck deformity **Investigations** • X ray to identify avulsion fracture and joint involvement **Management** • DIP joint splinting in extension • Continuous splinting for the prescribed period • Maintain PIP mobility • Surgical treatment in selected large / displaced fractures or joint instability • Physiotherapy splint compliance PIP / MCP mobility gradual DIP ROM after immobilization and progressive strengthening --- **9 Carpal Tunnel Syndrome** **Definition** Carpal tunnel syndrome is a compressive neuropathy of the median nerve at the wrist as it passes through the carpal tunnel **Causes / Risk Factors** • Repetitive wrist use • Diabetes • Pregnancy • Rheumatoid arthritis • Hypothyroidism • Wrist trauma • Space occupying lesions • Increased pressure within carpal tunnel **Clinical Features** • Numbness / tingling in thumb index middle and radial half of ring finger • Night symptoms • Hand weakness • Dropping objects • Difficulty with fine motor activities • Thenar muscle wasting in advanced cases • Reduced thumb opposition • Positive Phalen test • Positive Tinel sign may occur **Investigations** • Nerve conduction studies / EMG • Ultrasound • Imaging if structural pathology is suspected **Management** • Activity modification • Neutral wrist splint especially at night • Analgesics • Corticosteroid injection in selected cases • Median nerve gliding and tendon gliding exercises • Ergonomic correction • Wrist ROM • Carpal tunnel release for severe or persistent cases --- **10 Dupuytrens Contracture** **Definition** Dupuytrens contracture is a progressive fibrotic disorder of the palmar fascia producing nodules and cords that cause flexion contractures of the fingers most commonly ring and little fingers **Clinical Features** • Palmar nodules • Palmar cords • Progressive finger flexion contracture • Difficulty placing hand flat on a table • Difficulty washing face wearing gloves or putting hand into pockets • Usually painless • Reduced hand function **Management** • Observation in mild disease • Splinting in selected cases • ROM stretching and functional training • Postoperative rehabilitation • Needle fasciotomy / collagenase treatment in selected settings • Surgical fasciectomy for significant contracture --- **PELVIS AND HIP** --- **11 Iliotibial Band Syndrome** **Definition** IT band syndrome is an overuse condition causing pain around the lateral aspect of the knee resulting from irritation of tissues between the iliotibial band and lateral femoral region during repetitive knee flexion and extension **Causes** • Running • Cycling • Repetitive knee flexion extension • Sudden increase in training • Poor biomechanics • Weak hip abductors **Clinical Features** • Lateral knee pain • Pain during running especially downhill • Pain worsens with repetitive activity • Tenderness over lateral femoral epicondyle • Pain around 20 30 of knee flexion may occur • Tight IT band • Hip abductor weakness **Management** • Relative rest • Ice • Activity modification • Correct training errors • Stretching • Hip abductor and gluteal strengthening • Core strengthening • Running biomechanics correction • Gradual return to sport --- **12 Piriformis Syndrome** **Definition** Piriformis syndrome is a condition in which the piriformis muscle irritates or compresses the sciatic nerve producing buttock pain with possible radiating symptoms **Clinical Features** • Deep buttock pain • Pain may radiate down posterior thigh • Pain aggravated by prolonged sitting • Pain during running / walking • Tenderness over piriformis • Pain with hip flexion adduction and internal rotation • Possible paresthesia • Reduced tolerance for sitting **Management** • Activity modification • Ice / heat as appropriate • Analgesics • Piriformis stretching • Hip mobility exercises • Gluteal and core strengthening • Neural mobilization when appropriate • Postural correction • Gradual return to activity --- **13 Trochanteric Bursitis / Greater Trochanteric Pain Syndrome** **Definition** Trochanteric bursitis is pain around the greater trochanter traditionally attributed to inflammation of the trochanteric bursa Clinically lateral hip pain often represents greater trochanteric pain syndrome which can also involve the gluteal tendons **Clinical Features** • Lateral hip pain • Tenderness over greater trochanter • Pain when lying on affected side • Pain during walking / running • Difficulty climbing stairs • Pain during prolonged standing • Pain with resisted hip abduction • Possible IT band tightness **Management** • Avoid prolonged pressure on affected side • Activity modification • Ice • Analgesics / NSAIDs • Hip abductor gluteal and pelvic stabilization exercises • Flexibility work where appropriate • Gait retraining • Gradual functional progression • Injection may be considered in selected cases --- **KNEE** --- **14 Osteochondritis Dissecans** **Definition** Osteochondritis dissecans is a condition in which a segment of subchondral bone and its overlying articular cartilage becomes damaged and may become partially or completely separated from the surrounding bone **Common Site** Lateral aspect of medial femoral condyle is common **Causes / Risk Factors** • Repetitive microtrauma • Sports activities • Abnormal joint loading • Vascular factors may contribute **Clinical Features** • Activity related knee pain • Swelling / effusion • Tenderness • Catching or locking in unstable lesions • Reduced ROM • Limp • Quadriceps weakness • Mechanical symptoms in advanced disease **Investigations** • X ray • MRI • CT in selected cases **Management** • Activity restriction • Avoid impact loading • Physiotherapy • Maintain ROM • Quadriceps and hip strengthening • Surgical options in selected lesions drilling fixation debridement and osteochondral grafting --- **15 Prepatellar Bursitis** **Definition** Prepatellar bursitis is inflammation of the bursa located between the patella and overlying skin commonly caused by repetitive kneeling It is also called housemaids knee **Clinical Features** • Swelling directly over patella • Anterior knee pain / tenderness • Fluctuant swelling • Pain with kneeling • Redness / warmth in acute inflammation or infection • Knee joint ROM may be relatively preserved **Management** • Avoid kneeling • Knee padding • Ice • Compression • Analgesics • Aspiration when indicated • Antibiotics for septic bursitis • Physiotherapy after acute symptoms settle • Strengthening and functional rehabilitation --- **16 Suprapatellar Bursitis** **Definition** Suprapatellar bursitis is inflammation of the suprapatellar bursa located above the patella and communicating with the knee joint in many individuals **Clinical Features** • Swelling above patella • Anterior knee pain • Tenderness • Pain during knee flexion • Possible joint effusion • Reduced knee movement due to pain / swelling **Management** • Rest / activity modification • Ice • Compression • Analgesics / NSAIDs • Treatment of underlying inflammatory / infective cause • ROM exercises • Quadriceps strengthening • Gradual functional training --- **17 Popliteal Tendinitis** **Definition** Popliteal tendinitis is an overuse injury of the popliteus tendon producing posterolateral knee pain **Clinical Features** • Posterolateral knee pain • Pain during running especially downhill • Pain during knee flexion / rotation • Tenderness around posterolateral knee • Pain may increase with weight bearing • Possible feeling of instability **Management** • Relative rest • Ice • Activity modification • Analgesics as prescribed • Gentle ROM • Hamstring and calf flexibility • Progressive strengthening of knee and hip musculature • Proprioceptive training • Gradual return to running / sport --- **18 Patellar Tendinitis** **Definition** Patellar tendinitis / tendinopathy is an overuse disorder of the patellar tendon commonly at its attachment to the inferior pole of the patella It is commonly called jumpers knee **Causes** • Jumping sports • Running • Repetitive knee extension • Sudden increase in training • Poor load management **Clinical Features** • Anterior knee pain • Localized tenderness at inferior pole of patella • Pain during jumping running and squatting • Pain during stairs • Pain during resisted knee extension • Reduced functional performance **Management** • Activity modification and load management • Ice for symptomatic relief • Quadriceps stretching • Progressive tendon loading • Isometric quadriceps exercises initially • Eccentric or heavy slow resistance training • Hip / core strengthening • Functional and sports specific rehabilitation --- **19 Chondromalacia Patella** **Definition** Chondromalacia patella refers to softening and degenerative changes of the articular cartilage on the posterior surface of the patella commonly associated with patellofemoral pain and abnormal patellar loading **Clinical Features** • Anterior knee pain • Pain during stairs squatting running jumping or prolonged sitting • Crepitus • Patellar tenderness • Pain with appropriate patellar compression testing • Possible quadriceps and hip muscle weakness **Management** • Activity modification • Ice • Analgesics if prescribed • Quadriceps strengthening • Hip abductors / external rotator strengthening • Stretching of tight lower limb muscles • Patellar taping in selected patients • Movement retraining • Gradual return to activity --- **20 Plica Syndrome** **Definition** Plica syndrome is a symptomatic condition caused by irritation or thickening of a synovial plica usually the medial plica of the knee **Clinical Features** • Anterior / anteromedial knee pain • Clicking or snapping • Pain during repetitive knee flexion extension • Tenderness along medial patellar border • Pain during stairs / squatting • Possible catching sensation • Symptoms may increase after prolonged activity **Management** • Activity modification • Ice • Analgesics • Quadriceps and hip strengthening • Stretching • Patellar tracking / movement retraining • Gradual return to activity • Arthroscopic excision if persistent conservative treatment fails --- **21 Hoffas Fat Pad Syndrome** **Definition** Hoffas fat pad syndrome is a painful condition involving irritation inflammation or impingement of the infrapatellar Hoffas fat pad located behind the patellar tendon and below the patella **Causes** • Repetitive hyperextension • Direct trauma • Patellar maltracking • Repetitive loading • Previous knee surgery **Clinical Features** • Anterior knee pain • Pain around either side of patellar tendon • Pain aggravated by knee extension / hyperextension • Tenderness around infrapatellar region • Swelling / fullness • Pain during prolonged standing or activity • Possible catching **Management** • Avoid painful hyperextension • Activity modification • Ice • Analgesics as prescribed • Quadriceps and hip strengthening • Patellar control • Movement retraining • Flexibility exercises • Taping may be useful in selected patients • Persistent cases require orthopedic assessment --- **ANKLE AND FOOT** --- **22 Ankle Sprains** **Definition** An ankle sprain is an injury to one or more ligaments of the ankle caused by excessive or abnormal movement most commonly inversion injury affecting the lateral ligaments **Commonly Injured Ligaments** • Anterior talofibular ligament ATFL • Calcaneofibular ligament CFL • Posterior talofibular ligament **Clinical Features** • Pain • Swelling • Tenderness • Bruising • Difficulty weight bearing • Reduced ROM • Instability • Weakness • Severe injury may cause inability to walk normally **Grades** Grade I mild stretching / microscopic injury with mild pain and swelling Grade II partial ligament tear with moderate pain / swelling and some instability Grade III complete rupture with severe swelling and instability **Management** • Protection • Relative rest • Compression • Elevation • Early protected weight bearing as tolerated • Pain / swelling management • Gentle ROM • Progressive weight bearing • Calf and peroneal strengthening • Proprioception and balance training • Agility and sport specific rehabilitation • Bracing / taping where indicated --- **23 Plantar Fasciitis / Calcaneal Spur** **Definition** Plantar fasciitis is a degenerative / overuse condition of the plantar fascia commonly producing pain at its medial calcaneal attachment A calcaneal spur is a bony projection from the calcaneus and may be present with or without plantar fasciitis the spur itself is not necessarily the cause of pain **Risk Factors** • Prolonged standing • Running • Obesity • Tight calf muscles • Pes planus or abnormal foot mechanics • Sudden increase in activity • Inappropriate footwear **Clinical Features** • Heel pain especially plantar medial heel • First step pain after waking • Pain after prolonged sitting / rest • Pain after prolonged standing / walking • Tenderness at medial calcaneal tubercle • Tight calf / Achilles complex • Altered gait **Management** • Activity modification • Ice • Appropriate footwear • Heel cushioning • Calf stretching • Plantar fascia stretching • Foot intrinsic strengthening • Calf strengthening • Orthotics where indicated • Taping • Night splint in selected cases • Progressive loading • Weight management where appropriate • Persistent cases may require specialist treatment --- **24 Tarsal Tunnel Syndrome** **Definition** Tarsal tunnel syndrome is a compressive neuropathy of the tibial nerve or its branches as it passes through the tarsal tunnel behind the medial malleolus **Causes** • Trauma • Ankle sprain • Space occupying lesion • Ganglion • Diabetes • Inflammatory conditions • Foot deformity • Repetitive loading **Clinical Features** • Pain around medial ankle • Burning / tingling • Numbness in plantar foot • Symptoms may radiate toward sole / toes • Worse with prolonged standing / walking • Night symptoms may occur • Positive Tinel sign behind medial malleolus • Possible intrinsic foot muscle weakness **Investigations** • Clinical examination • Nerve conduction studies / EMG • Ultrasound / MRI where appropriate **Management** • Activity modification • Appropriate footwear • Orthotics if indicated • Analgesics • Neural mobilization • Calf stretching • Foot strengthening • Gait correction • Proprioceptive training • Treatment of underlying cause • Surgical decompression for persistent / severe compression --- **25 Achilles Tendinitis / Achilles Tendinopathy** **Definition** Achilles tendinopathy is a painful overuse disorder of the Achilles tendon usually involving degenerative changes associated with repetitive mechanical loading **Causes / Risk Factors** • Running and jumping • Sudden increase in training • Poor training progression • Tight calf muscles • Inappropriate footwear • Reduced ankle dorsiflexion • Previous tendon problems **Clinical Features** • Pain at Achilles tendon • Mid portion or insertional pain • Morning stiffness • Pain during running / jumping • Tenderness • Tendon thickening may occur • Pain during resisted plantarflexion • Reduced calf strength • Difficulty with repeated heel raises **Management** • Relative rest / load modification • Ice for symptom relief • Appropriate footwear • Heel lift temporarily in selected cases • Calf stretching where appropriate • Progressive tendon loading • Isometric exercises initially when painful • Heavy slow resistance / eccentric loading • Calf strengthening • Balance and functional training • Gradual return to running / jumping --- **26 Metatarsalgia** **Definition** Metatarsalgia is pain arising from the forefoot / metatarsal head region usually caused by excessive loading of the metatarsal heads **Causes** • Excessive running / jumping • Improper footwear • High heels • Pes cavus • Hallux valgus • Altered foot biomechanics • Increased body weight • Fat pad changes **Clinical Features** • Pain under metatarsal heads • Burning or aching forefoot pain • Pain worsens with walking / running • Feeling of walking on a pebble may occur • Callus formation • Tenderness • Reduced tolerance for prolonged standing **Management** • Activity modification • Proper footwear • Avoid high heels • Metatarsal pads • Orthotics when indicated • Ice • Calf and foot stretching • Foot intrinsic strengthening • Gait correction • Gradual return to activity • Treat underlying foot deformity --- **27 Mortons Neuroma** **Definition** Mortons neuroma is a painful perineural fibrotic enlargement / irritation of a common digital nerve most commonly occurring between the third and fourth toes **Causes / Risk Factors** • Narrow footwear • High heels • Repetitive forefoot loading • Running • Foot deformities **Clinical Features** • Burning forefoot pain • Pain radiating toward toes • Numbness / tingling • Feeling of a pebble or lump inside the shoe • Symptoms worsen with tight shoes • Pain increases during walking / running • Tenderness in interdigital space • Clicking may be produced by compression of the forefoot Mulder sign **Investigations** • Clinical examination • Ultrasound • MRI in selected cases **Management** • Wide toe box footwear • Avoid high heels • Metatarsal pads • Activity modification • Orthotics where indicated • Analgesics • Foot intrinsic strengthening • Calf stretching • Foot mobility • Gait correction • Injection may be considered • Surgical decompression / neurectomy in persistent cases","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm considering the best way to approach problem-solving using available tools. Specifically, I'm now focused on tool selection. My current thinking prioritizes using the most specific tool for a given task, such as a file viewer instead of a general terminal. I'm exploring how this impacts efficiency and accuracy in problem-solving.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Application**\n\nI've been reviewing the critical instructions regarding tool usage, particularly avoiding `cat` for file creation and `grep` within Bash. I'm focusing on the `grep_search` function. I'm also ensuring that I explicitly consider and list all potentially related tools *before* executing any tool set. I'm currently working to format a document according to these rules, while removing the extraneous \"Page [X] BPT University Exam Notes\" text.\n\n\n**Implem