traction in selected cases Soft cervical collar for short-term use when appropriate Botulinum toxin for selected dystonic torticollis SURGICAL MANAGEMENT Indicated when severe fixed deformity or structural pathology persists • • • Sternocleidomastoid release/lengthening Muscle/tendon release Cervical decompression or stabilization when indicated by underlying pathology FLOW CHART Underlying cause → muscle spasm/shortening → abnormal head position → restricted ROM → chronic deformity → functional limitation 2. ACQUIRED SCOLIOSIS DEFINITION Scoliosis is a three-dimensional deformity of the spine characterized by lateral curvature associated with vertebral rotation, generally measured by the Cobb angle on radiographs. Acquired scoliosis develops after birth due to a known or secondary cause. BPT / Orthopaedics — University Exam Answers Page 2 of 20CAUSES Poor posture Leg-length discrepancy Muscle imbalance Cerebral palsy Muscular dystrophy Poliomyelitis Spinal tuberculosis Vertebral fracture Tumors Degenerative spinal disease Painful conditions Trauma CLINICAL FEATURES Unequal shoulder height Unequal scapular prominence Unequal waist contours Pelvic asymmetry Lateral spinal curvature Rib prominence Trunk shift Back pain, particularly in degenerative scoliosis Reduced spinal mobility Leg-length discrepancy may be present CLINICAL TEST Adam’s forward-bending test Patient bends forward. Rib hump or lumbar prominence suggests vertebral rotation. COMPLICATIONS Chronic back pain Progressive deformity Reduced pulmonary function in severe curves Reduced exercise tolerance Neurological compromise in selected structural causes Psychological/social problems Functional disability RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES Standing AP/PA and lateral spine radiographs demonstrate: Lateral curvature Vertebral rotation Cobb angle Vertebral wedging Degenerative changes where applicable CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT Treat underlying cause • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • BPT / Orthopaedics — University Exam Answers Page 3 of 20• • • • • • • • • Physiotherapy Postural correction Core strengthening Spinal mobility exercises Stretching Breathing exercises Bracing in selected growing patients Leg-length correction if discrepancy contributes Analgesics for pain SURGICAL MANAGEMENT Indications depend on severity, progression, symptoms and cause. • • • • Spinal instrumentation and fusion Corrective osteotomy in selected rigid deformities Decompression if neurological compression exists Treatment of underlying tumor/infection when responsible FLOW CHART Underlying condition → muscle imbalance/vertebral pathology → lateral curvature + rotation → progressive deformity → pain/functional impairment 3. KYPHOSIS DEFINITION Kyphosis is an excessive posterior convex curvature of the thoracic or thoracolumbar spine, producing an exaggerated rounded-back appearance. CAUSES • • • • • • • • • • Poor posture Osteoporosis Vertebral compression fractures Scheuermann disease Tuberculosis of spine Degenerative disease Trauma Tumor Neuromuscular disorders Ankylosing spondylitis CLINICAL FEATURES • • • • • • Rounded back Forward head posture Increased thoracic curvature Shoulder protraction Back pain Hamstring tightness may occur BPT / Orthopaedics — University Exam Answers Page 4 of 20• • • • Reduced spinal mobility Reduced chest expansion in severe cases Fatigue Neurological symptoms in severe structural disease COMPLICATIONS • • • • • • • Chronic pain Progressive deformity Restrictive respiratory impairment Reduced balance Increased fall risk Neurological compression Spinal cord injury in severe cases RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES • • • • • Increased thoracic kyphotic angle Vertebral wedging Compression fractures Degenerative changes Vertebral destruction in infective disease CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT • • • • • • • • • Postural education Thoracic extension exercises Back extensor strengthening Abdominal strengthening Stretching of pectoral muscles Breathing exercises Analgesics Osteoporosis management Bracing in selected patients SURGICAL MANAGEMENT • • • • Vertebral augmentation in selected painful compression fractures Corrective osteotomy Spinal fusion Decompression and stabilization when neurological compression/instability is present 4. LORDOSIS DEFINITION Lordosis is an excessive anterior convex curvature of the spine, most commonly affecting the lumbar region. CAUSES • • • • Obesity Pregnancy Weak abdominal muscles Tight hip flexors BPT / Orthopaedics — University Exam Answers Page 5 of 20• • • • • • Spondylolisthesis Hip flexion deformity Osteoporosis Spinal deformity Poor posture Neuromuscular disorders CLINICAL FEATURES • • • • • • • • Increased lumbar curvature Anterior pelvic tilt Protruding abdomen Prominent buttocks Low-back pain Tight hip flexors Weak abdominal/gluteal muscles Hamstring dysfunction may occur COMPLICATIONS • • • • • • Chronic low-back pain Facet joint stress Muscle fatigue Reduced mobility Secondary disc/facet degeneration Neurological symptoms if associated with spinal pathology CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT • • • • • • • • • Postural correction Abdominal strengthening Gluteal strengthening Hip-flexor stretching Hamstring stretching when appropriate Core stabilization Weight management Ergonomic modification Analgesia SURGICAL MANAGEMENT Surgery is directed at the underlying cause: • • • • Decompression Spinal stabilization/fusion Corrective osteotomy Treatment of spondylolisthesis when indicated Page 6 of 20 BPT / Orthopaedics — University Exam Answers5. GENU VARUM DEFINITION Genu varum, or bow legs, is a deformity in which the knees remain separated when the ankles are brought together, producing an outward angulation of the legs. CAUSES • • • • • • • • Physiological deformity Rickets Blount disease Malunited fractures Growth-plate injury Osteoarthritis Skeletal dysplasia Obesity in some cases CLINICAL FEATURES • • • • • • Bowing of legs Knees separated when ankles touch Altered gait Lateral thrust at knee Knee pain in severe cases Unequal loading of knee joint COMPLICATIONS • • • • • Medial compartment knee overload Early osteoarthritis Patellofemoral dysfunction Gait abnormalities Progressive deformity RADIOLOGY • • • • Increased medial angulation Assessment of mechanical axis Growth-plate abnormalities where applicable Degenerative changes in adults CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT • • • • • • Treat underlying metabolic disease Weight management Physiotherapy Muscle strengthening Gait training Orthoses in selected cases SURGICAL MANAGEMENT For significant persistent/progressive deformity: • • Guided growth/hemiepiphysiodesis in growing children Corrective osteotomy in mature patients BPT / Orthopaedics — University Exam Answers Page 7 of 20• Knee replacement for severe degenerative disease 6. GENU VALGUM DEFINITION Genu valgum, or knock-knee deformity, is a condition in which the knees approximate or touch while the ankles remain separated. CAUSES • • • • • • • • Physiological childhood valgus Rickets Obesity Growth-plate injury Malunion Skeletal dysplasia Osteoarthritis Previous trauma CLINICAL FEATURES • • • • • • Knees come together Ankles remain apart Altered gait Patellar maltracking Knee pain Difficulty running/walking in severe cases COMPLICATIONS • • • • • Patellofemoral instability Lateral compartment overload Early osteoarthritis Gait abnormalities Functional limitation CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT • • • • • Treatment of underlying cause Weight management Hip and knee strengthening Gait training Orthoses in selected cases SURGICAL MANAGEMENT • • • • Guided growth Distal femoral osteotomy Corrective osteotomy Joint replacement in severe degenerative disease Page 8 of 20 BPT / Orthopaedics — University Exam Answers7. GENU RECURVATUM DEFINITION Genu recurvatum is a deformity in which the knee is excessively extended beyond the normal physiological range. CAUSES • • • • • • • • • Ligamentous laxity Neuromuscular disorders Poliomyelitis Quadriceps weakness Anterior capsular laxity Trauma Malunion Abnormal tibial slope Poor gait mechanics CLINICAL FEATURES • • • • • • • Hyperextension of knee Posterior displacement of knee during standing Knee pain Instability Abnormal gait Quadriceps weakness Difficulty controlling knee during stance COMPLICATIONS • • • • • • Chronic ligament strain Knee instability Pain Degenerative changes Meniscal injury Gait dysfunction CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT • • • • • • Quadriceps and hamstring strengthening Proprioceptive training Gait retraining Knee brace/orthosis Correction of underlying neuromuscular disorder Activity modification SURGICAL MANAGEMENT • • • • Ligament reconstruction where indicated Corrective osteotomy Growth modulation in selected children Other reconstruction based on structural cause BPT / Orthopaedics — University Exam Answers Page 9 of 208. COXA VARA DEFINITION Coxa vara is a deformity in which the femoral neck-shaft angle is reduced, generally below approximately 120° in adults. CAUSES • • • • • • • Congenital/developmental conditions Rickets Trauma Fracture malunion Fibrous dysplasia Perthes disease Metabolic bone disease CLINICAL FEATURES • • • • • • • Shortening of limb Limp Trendelenburg gait Reduced hip abduction Apparent shortening Hip pain Muscle weakness COMPLICATIONS • • • • • Progressive deformity Hip instability Early osteoarthritis Limb-length discrepancy Abductor weakness RADIOLOGY • • • Reduced neck-shaft angle Shortened/vertical femoral neck Altered femoral head mechanics CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT • • • • • Treat underlying metabolic disorder Physiotherapy Hip abductor strengthening Gait training Activity modification SURGICAL MANAGEMENT • • • Corrective valgus osteotomy Internal fixation Treatment of underlying bone pathology BPT / Orthopaedics — University Exam Answers Page 10 of 209. COXA VALGA DEFINITION Coxa valga is a deformity in which the femoral neck-shaft angle is increased, generally above approximately 135° in adults. CAUSES • • • • • • Neuromuscular disorders Developmental abnormalities Hip dysplasia Cerebral palsy Muscle imbalance Certain metabolic/skeletal disorders CLINICAL FEATURES • • • • • • Reduced hip stability Limp Abductor weakness Limited hip mechanics Possible hip pain Increased tendency toward subluxation in susceptible patients COMPLICATIONS • • • • • Hip instability Subluxation/dislocation Abductor insufficiency Altered gait Early degenerative changes MANAGEMENT • • • • • • Treat underlying condition Hip strengthening Gait training Orthotic management when indicated Corrective femoral osteotomy in significant symptomatic deformity Hip reconstruction when associated with instability/dysplasia 10. PES CAVUS DEFINITION Pes cavus is a foot deformity characterized by an abnormally high medial longitudinal arch, often associated with plantarflexion of the first ray, hindfoot varus and clawing of the toes. CAUSES • • Neuromuscular disorders Hereditary motor-sensory neuropathy BPT / Orthopaedics — University Exam Answers Page 11 of 20• • • • • Poliomyelitis Cerebral palsy Spinal cord disorders Trauma Idiopathic causes CLINICAL FEATURES • • • • • • • • • High medial arch Shortened foot appearance Hindfoot varus Claw toes Prominent metatarsal heads Lateral border weight bearing Callosities Foot pain Ankle instability COMPLICATIONS • • • • • • • Metatarsalgia Calluses Ankle sprains Plantar fascia pain Difficulty wearing shoes Progressive deformity Gait abnormality CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT • • • • • • • • Treat underlying neurological disease Custom orthoses Appropriate footwear Stretching Strengthening Balance training Gait correction Pressure redistribution SURGICAL MANAGEMENT • • • • • • Plantar fascia release in selected flexible deformities Tendon transfer Calcaneal osteotomy First-ray osteotomy Midfoot osteotomy Arthrodesis for rigid severe deformity Page 12 of 20 BPT / Orthopaedics — University Exam Answers11. PES PLANUS DEFINITION Pes planus, or flat foot, is a condition in which the medial longitudinal arch of the foot is reduced or absent, often accompanied by excessive hindfoot valgus and pronation. CAUSES • • • • • • • • Ligamentous laxity Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction Obesity Trauma Rheumatoid arthritis Neuromuscular disorders Degenerative changes Tarsal coalition CLINICAL FEATURES • • • • • • • • • Flattened medial arch Excessive pronation Hindfoot valgus Heel eversion Forefoot abduction Foot fatigue Medial foot/ankle pain Difficulty prolonged standing/walking Abnormal shoe wear COMPLICATIONS • • • • • • Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction Plantar fasciitis Ankle pain Knee/hip biomechanical problems Progressive deformity Difficulty walking CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT • • • • • • • • • Weight management Footwear modification Arch-support orthoses Heel support Stretching of tight calf muscles Foot intrinsic strengthening Posterior tibial muscle strengthening Balance and gait training Activity modification BPT / Orthopaedics — University Exam Answers Page 13 of 20SURGICAL MANAGEMENT For symptomatic rigid/progressive deformity: • • • • • Tendon reconstruction Calcaneal osteotomy Midfoot reconstruction Arthrodesis for rigid deformity Procedures addressing the underlying cause 12. HALLUX RIGIDUS DEFINITION Hallux rigidus is a degenerative arthritic condition of the first metatarsophalangeal joint characterized predominantly by painful restriction of great-toe dorsiflexion. CAUSES • • • • • • Degenerative osteoarthritis Previous trauma Repetitive loading Anatomical abnormalities Inflammatory arthritis Previous surgery CLINICAL FEATURES • • • • • • • • Pain at first MTP joint Restricted dorsiflexion Difficulty during push-off Dorsal osteophyte Swelling Stiffness Altered gait Shoe discomfort COMPLICATIONS • • • • • Progressive joint degeneration Gait alteration Transfer metatarsalgia Callus formation Reduced walking tolerance RADIOLOGY • • • • • Joint-space narrowing Dorsal osteophytes Subchondral sclerosis Cystic changes Advanced joint destruction Page 14 of 20 BPT / Orthopaedics — University Exam AnswersCONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT • • • • • • • • Activity modification NSAIDs/analgesics Stiff-soled shoes Rocker-bottom footwear Orthoses Physiotherapy ROM exercises within tolerance Intra-articular injection in selected cases SURGICAL MANAGEMENT • • • • Cheilectomy for selected early disease Osteotomy in selected patients First MTP arthrodesis for advanced disease Joint replacement in selected patients 13. HALLUX VALGUS DEFINITION Hallux valgus is a progressive deformity in which the great toe deviates laterally toward the lesser toes with medial deviation of the first metatarsal, producing a prominent medial eminence or bunion. CAUSES/RISK FACTORS • • • • • • • Genetic predisposition Footwear with narrow toe box Ligamentous laxity Pes planus Abnormal first-ray mechanics Rheumatoid arthritis Neuromuscular disorders CLINICAL FEATURES • • • • • • • • • Lateral deviation of great toe Medial bunion Pain Redness and pressure over bunion Difficulty wearing shoes Callosity Reduced first MTP movement Transfer metatarsalgia Lesser-toe deformity COMPLICATIONS • • • • Bursitis Osteoarthritis Metatarsalgia Hammer/claw toes BPT / Orthopaedics — University Exam Answers Page 15 of 20• • Skin ulceration Persistent gait disturbance RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES Weight-bearing X-ray may show: • • • • • Increased hallux valgus angle Increased intermetatarsal angle Medial deviation of first metatarsal Subluxation of first MTP joint Degenerative changes CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT • • • • • • • • • Wide toe-box footwear Avoid tight/high-heeled shoes Bunion pads Orthoses Toe spacers in selected cases Analgesics Physiotherapy Calf/foot stretching and strengthening Activity modification SURGICAL MANAGEMENT Indicated for persistent pain and functional limitation despite conservative treatment. • • • • • • Distal metatarsal osteotomy Proximal metatarsal osteotomy Lapidus procedure in selected patients Akin osteotomy First MTP arthrodesis for severe arthritis/deformity Bunion correction according to deformity severity 14. HAMMER TOE DEFINITION Hammer toe is a deformity of a lesser toe characterized by flexion deformity of the proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joint, often with relative extension at the MTP and DIP joints. CAUSES • • • • • • • • Tight footwear Muscle imbalance Hallux valgus Pes cavus Rheumatoid arthritis Neurological disorders Tendon imbalance Trauma BPT / Orthopaedics — University Exam Answers Page 16 of 20CLINICAL FEATURES • • • • • • • • Flexed PIP joint Pain over prominent PIP joint Corn formation Callosity Difficulty wearing shoes MTP pain Reduced toe mobility Gait difficulty in severe cases COMPLICATIONS • • • • • • • Corns Ulceration Infection MTP instability Metatarsalgia Fixed deformity Difficulty walking CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT • • • • • • • • Wide toe-box shoes Avoid tight footwear Toe pads Orthoses Splinting/taping in flexible deformity Toe stretching Intrinsic muscle strengthening Callus care SURGICAL MANAGEMENT For painful fixed deformity: • • • • • Tendon release/transfer PIP joint arthroplasty PIP arthrodesis MTP procedures when required Correction of associated hallux valgus/pes cavus 15. METATARSALGIA DEFINITION Metatarsalgia is a clinical condition characterized by pain and tenderness beneath the metatarsal heads, usually caused by excessive or abnormal loading of the forefoot. CAUSES • • • Excessive activity/running Improper footwear High heels BPT / Orthopaedics — University Exam Answers Page 17 of 20Pes cavus Pes planus Hallux valgus Claw/hammer toes Short or long metatarsals Fat-pad atrophy Stress fracture Inflammatory arthritis CLINICAL FEATURES Pain beneath metatarsal heads Burning or aching sensation Pain worsens during walking/running Forefoot tenderness Callosity Feeling of walking on a pebble Reduced walking tolerance COMPLICATIONS Chronic pain Altered gait Callus formation Lesser-toe deformity Reduced physical activity Secondary knee/hip biomechanical problems INVESTIGATIONS Clinical examination Weight-bearing X-ray Ultrasound/MRI when neuroma, stress fracture or soft-tissue pathology is suspected CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT Activity modification Rest during painful period Proper footwear with wide toe box Metatarsal pads Custom orthoses Shock-absorbing insoles Calf and foot stretching Foot intrinsic strengthening Weight management Analgesics/NSAIDs when appropriate SURGICAL MANAGEMENT Surgery is considered when persistent symptoms fail conservative treatment or when a structural cause is identified. Metatarsal osteotomy Correction of associated hallux valgus Correction of lesser-toe deformity Excision of neuroma where appropriate\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-13T16:24:19+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
